go vet ./...
go test -v -count=1 ./...
```

Verified output:

```
=== RUN   TestBuggyBlowsPastBudget
    retry_test.go:41: buggy: elapsed=1.004098084s
        err=Get "http://<ip-address>:39803": context deadline exceeded
        (Client.Timeout exceeded while awaiting headers)
--- PASS: TestBuggyBlowsPastBudget (1.00s)
=== RUN   TestFixedHonorsSharedBudget
    retry_test.go:76: fixed: elapsed=351.516335ms
        err=retry budget exhausted: Get "http://<ip-address>:37891": context deadline exceeded
--- PASS: TestFixedHonorsSharedBudget (0.35s)
=== RUN   TestFixedSucceedsWithinBudget
--- PASS: TestFixedSucceedsWithinBudget (0.10s)
PASS
ok  	example.com/retryfix	1.460s
```

| Test | Before | After |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TestBuggyBlowsPastBudget` | 4 retries × 200 ms `Client.Timeout` = **1.00 s**, exact `Client.Timeout exceeded` error | — |
| `TestFixedHonorsSharedBudget` | — | **351 ms**, bounded by the 400 ms shared budget, returns `ErrBudgetExhausted` |
| `TestFixedSucceedsWithinBudget` | — | 200 for a fast server, no false timeout |

The package-level budget assertion `elapsed <= budget + 75ms` makes the regression impossible to reintroduce: the sum of all attempts and backoffs can never exceed the caller's deadline.

---

**Bottom line:** remove `http.Client.Timeout` from retried clients, put a single `context.WithTimeout` around the whole operation, derive each attempt from it, and make backoff cancellable/deadline-aware. That converts `N × timeout` worst-case latency into a hard `budget` upper bound.
